And then in a new Yahoo News YouGov poll,
Democrats, 45.

00:43:08.800 --> 00:43:10.690
Republicans, 38.

00:43:10.690 --> 00:43:13.840
That's plus seven for Democrats.

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:15.880
Why are these numbers such a big deal?

00:43:15.880 --> 00:43:17.050
There's two reasons.

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:22.470
Number one, this is not typically what you
see when one party has taken the White House

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:27.060
in the previous election, in 2020, Democrats
took the White House from Republicans.

00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:28.690
Biden beat Trump.

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:30.110
You all know that.

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:34.380
And typically, almost always with like three
exceptions.

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:38.550
When that happens, you would expect Republicans
to do really well in the first midterms, which

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:40.500
would be four and a half months from now in
November.

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:45.270
So, number one, the fact that the numbers
differ from that traditional historical reality

00:43:45.270 --> 00:43:46.760
is a really big deal.

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:50.670
But the second reason that it's a really big
deal is this is not the way the numbers look

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:52.280
just three months ago.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:59.670
And I think that we can all sort of take a
cynical approach here and say this is temporary.

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:03.050
It'll go back to Republicans crushing Democrats
by November.

00:44:03.050 --> 00:44:07.150
Well, it's not obvious to me, but one of the
things we've been talking about now for about

00:44:07.150 --> 00:44:14.030
two months is, are there unique circumstances
in this election that make it potentially

00:44:14.030 --> 00:44:16.800
okay for Democrats when Republicans should
be winning?

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:18.440
And I say that the answer is yes.

00:44:18.440 --> 00:44:19.820
This is not a guarantee of anything.

00:44:19.820 --> 00:44:23.970
I'm still getting a lot of people on Twitter
saying, David, you loser.

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:26.220
Some of them say worse things than that.

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:28.220
Republicans are going to crush Democrats in
November.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:29.280
Just wait and see.

00:44:29.280 --> 00:44:33.260
Well, maybe, but there are some different
circumstances here.

00:44:33.260 --> 00:44:41.200
First of all, normally when we say, oh, Democrats
can win by running on abortion rights, know

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:44.360
that historically that's not a good issue
for Democrats to run on.

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:45.360
It's not a winning issue.

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:46.800
It's not a voting issue for Democrats.

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:51.850
It's the type of issue that will motivate
Republicans to come out to vote against a

00:44:51.850 --> 00:44:53.369
pro-choice candidate.

00:44:53.369 --> 00:44:57.970
Typically, that's the way it is this time
around, it looks like it could be different.

00:44:57.970 --> 00:45:02.840
Americans are more in favor of abortion being
legal in most cases than at any time over

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:04.280
the last 50 years.

00:45:04.280 --> 00:45:08.110
So public opinion is different now than it
has been on abortion.

00:45:08.110 --> 00:45:15.430
And in addition to that, Republican appointed
Republican nominee Trump nominated Supreme

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:22.300
Court justices have overturn Roe v Wade, something
which two thirds of Americans are in disagreement

00:45:22.300 --> 00:45:23.400
with.

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:24.570
That is different.

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:31.970
And that means in this election, that situation
could make it better for Democrats, particularly

00:45:31.970 --> 00:45:36.190
if they say we're the ones trying to keep
these rights, whether it's credible or not,

00:45:36.190 --> 00:45:38.320
that that's what Democrats are going to do.

00:45:38.320 --> 00:45:43.200
Secondly, normally running on gun safety regulation
is a losing issue for Democrats.

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:44.200
We've talked about it before.

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:46.550
We've talked about it with a number of political
strategists.

00:45:46.550 --> 00:45:52.420
When Democrats make gun safety regulations
a big part of their campaign, it doesn't really

00:45:52.420 --> 00:45:57.110
motivate Democrats to come out and vote for
them, but it does motivate Republicans to

00:45:57.110 --> 00:45:59.120
come out and vote against them.

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:04.340
However, the rash of school and other mass
shootings in the United States over the last

00:46:04.340 --> 00:46:09.360
three months and just tragic numbers, hundreds
of mass shootings in the United States in

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:18.530
2022 so far seem to have tired out and exhausted
many Americans about the Republican Party's

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:22.750
unwillingness to consider serious gun safety
regulations.

00:46:22.750 --> 00:46:27.290
And so you have a situation where in this
particular midterm, it might actually work

00:46:27.290 --> 00:46:31.310
for Democrats to run on gun safety and on
abortion rights.

00:46:31.310 --> 00:46:32.310
So that's.

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:33.700
The normal.

00:46:33.700 --> 00:46:38.810
The other element that is different than normal
in these midterms is that we are coming up

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:43.380
against the tail end of this six year Trumpian
cycle.

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:46.320
And I'll explain to you what I mean by that.

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:53.340
Normally, you have a sort of relatively similar
electorate throughout three elections.

00:46:53.340 --> 00:47:01.150
So like if you look at, I don't know, 2000,
2002 and 2004, as an example, the electorate

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:03.870
George W Bush won the presidency in 2000.

00:47:03.870 --> 00:47:05.670
And then you had the 2002 midterms.

00:47:05.670 --> 00:47:08.450
And Bush won reelection in 2004.

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:14.310
There was no major change to the electorate
in the year 2000, which then had to sort of

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:16.619
sort itself out in 2000, two.

00:47:16.619 --> 00:47:19.100
And in 2004, it was a very similar electorate.

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:20.100
Okay.

00:47:20.100 --> 00:47:21.930
That is not the case right now.

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:30.160
In 2016, you had all of these formerly nonpolitical,
nonvoting people who were enamored with Trump,

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:34.880
a lot of low information voters, a lot of
extremists of different kinds, people who

00:47:34.880 --> 00:47:35.990
